7. ANALYSIS

The available evidence indicates a phishing campaign using a deceptive offer to encourage the recipient to download a malicious archive. The email was sent from free@coffeeshooop.com with the subject "Free Coffee Voucher" and contained a link to a ZIP file hosted on an Amazon S3 domain.

The reported failures of DNS, SPF, and DKIM checks, together with the suspicious sender IP and malicious file reputation, support the assessment that the email was malicious. The email gateway allowed the message to be delivered, giving the recipient an opportunity to interact with the link.

The playbook findings indicate that the recipient's host accessed the URL, downloaded the archive, and executed the malware. This is more serious than an attempted phishing email that was never opened or executed. The analyst note states that the file was not opened, but this conflicts with the playbook findings that the file was downloaded and executed. The discrepancy must be resolved by reviewing endpoint and process logs.

If execution is confirmed, the affected endpoint must be treated as potentially compromised. The investigation should establish the malware's behavior, whether credentials were exposed, whether persistence was established, and whether any command-and-control communication or data exfiltration occurred.

Immediate endpoint containment, malicious email removal, indicator blocking, and a review of other recipients are recommended. The incident should remain open until the execution findings and scope of compromise have been established.

